# PLAN-6: Lift the encoder ceiling — client supervised auxiliary loss (Phase 3A)

## Current evidence
- Proxy (CIFAR-100, seed 20260714, 10 rounds, test-as-val): SplitFL 44.57%, Federated 34.76%.
- H-SFP raw baseline: 3.81%. `centered_cosine` (PLAN-5): best 3.48% — NEUTRAL in-pipeline (within
  noise; the static-diagnostic 21% was a cosine head on REAL features, not the synthetic path).
- Diagnostic ceilings (round-1 encoder, cached real feats): linear-probe 30% (L1)/26% (L2);
  best prototype oracle whitened NC ~25%, whitened synthetic head ~16–17%. → Even a perfect
  prototype fix cannot reach 44% on THIS encoder.
- Clients train SELF-SUPERVISED (SSL loss ~0.9). SplitFL trains supervised end-to-end → 44%.
- CONCLUSION (matches PLAN-3's own decision rule "if real→real poor, fix encoder first"): the
  ENCODER representation is the primary ceiling; prototype geometry is a secondary refinement.
- `prototype_space` flag exists (raw default bitwise-identical; centered_cosine comms-free). Keep it.

## Hypothesis (falsifiable)
The H-SFP client encoder is under-trained because it is purely self-supervised on ~250 samples/
client. Adding a legitimate SUPERVISED auxiliary signal at the client (labels are already available
in standard FL) — supervised-contrastive and/or a local linear-classification CE on the client
feature — will raise the encoder's LINEAR-PROBE ceiling from ~30% toward the baseline range, and
raise end-to-end H-SFP proxy val well above 3.81%. Prediction: encoder linear-probe (measured on the
trained client encoder) ≥ 40%, and end-to-end val meaningfully > 3.81%.

## Proposed isolated change
Add an ablation flag `client_objective` ∈ {ssl (default = current), ssl_supcon, supervised}:
- `ssl` unchanged (default; preserves current H-SFP behavior for ablation).
- `ssl_supcon`: current SSL + a lightweight supervised-contrastive loss on client features (labels
  local to the client); small weight.
- `supervised`: add a local linear CE head on the client feature (classic supervised rep learning),
  optionally alongside SSL.
No sample-level cross-tier transfer changes; prototypes/comms path unchanged.

## Invariants
- `ssl` default MUST reproduce current H-SFP exactly (regression test).
- No sample-level activation/gradient transfer between tiers; the supervised signal is LOCAL to the
  client (its own labels), consistent with standard FL. Record any comms/compute delta (expected
  ~0 comms; small client compute).
- Preserve uncommitted changes + results; unique output dirs. Codex implements + CPU tests; Claude
  runs on host GPU. Do not modify baselines/default configs (flag default off).

## Codex implementation tasks
1. Implement `client_objective` flag + the supcon/supervised client losses (small, configurable
   weight) in the classification H-SFP client training path. Keep memory/reliability/PRC/dropout and
   `prototype_space` orthogonal/compatible.
2. Add an ENCODER LINEAR-PROBE hook/utility that, at end of a run (or via oracle_probe on the run's
   checkpoint), reports the client-encoder linear-probe top-1 on real features — so we can measure
   whether the ceiling rose above the round-1 30%.
3. CPU unit tests: (a) `ssl` regression bitwise-unchanged; (b) supcon/supervised losses are finite,
   nonzero, and produce gradients into the client encoder; (c) losses are LOCAL (no cross-tier
   tensors).
4. Prepare + emit EXACT host-GPU commands for the proxy: baseline_hsfp with
   client_objective={ssl (control), supervised} on the SAME partition/seed/10-round budget, plus the
   linear-probe measurement command for each resulting checkpoint. Record the failed/neutral
